Factorizer.java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class Factorizer {

    public ArrayList<Integer> factorize(int number) {

        ArrayList<Integer> factors = new ArrayList<>();

        if (number <= 1) {
            return factors;
        }

        int divisor = 2;

        while (number > 1) {

            if (number % divisor == 0) {
                factors.add(divisor);
                number = number / divisor;
            } else {
                divisor++;
            }
        }

        return factors;
    }
}






Main.java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Factorizer factorizer = new Factorizer();

        System.out.println(factorizer.factorize(100));
        System.out.println(factorizer.factorize(35));
        System.out.println(factorizer.factorize(24));
        System.out.println(factorizer.factorize(17));
        System.out.println(factorizer.factorize(1));
    }
}









1. I tested different kinds of numbers. I tested numbers like 100 and 24 that have many factors, a prime number like 17, and numbers like 1 and 0. This helped me make sure the program works in different situations.

